  "query_preview": "/MONDAY THROUGH NEXT FRIDAY/... \n\n\nModel uncertainty remains for the first half of next week,\nprimarily with respect to thunderstorm chances, as the position \nof the high is likely to set up somewhere over eastern New Mexico \nand western Texas by Monday. There is good global ensemble \nconsensus in a brief increase in moisture and instability Sunday \nevening into Monday, most notably across southwest AZ and \nsoutheast CA, but NBM forecast PoPs are mostly under 15% for the \nlower deserts both days. At this point, we could continue to see \nmostly slight chances favoring the high terrain into early next \nweek, but if moisture does eventually improve enough storm chances\nare likely to be higher than what the NBM is depicting. By \nTuesday, drier air will filter back into the region as the \nsubtropical high begins to retrograde back over our region. This \nwill limit low end rain chances almost exclusively to the higher \nterrain areas with the lower deserts remaining dry. By the latter \nhalf of next week, the NBM shows PoPs being confined to the \nMogollon Rim with no chances for our CWA. \n\nTemperatures are expected to continue to be more influenced by the \nstrong subtropical high as H5 heights hover between 593-596dm across \nthe Desert Southwest through early next week. Daytime highs are not \nexpected to change much from Sunday to Monday, barring any \nsignificant change with the convective chances. As mentioned above, \nby Tuesday the subtropical high will start to retrograde back to the \nwest and the center will eventually settle over AZ/NM during the \nlatter half of the workweek. The high is also expected to \nstrengthen to around 596-598dam during this time. The combination\nof the center settling over our region and strengthening will \nresult in temperatures heating up to around 10 degrees above \nnormal for this time of year. Afternoon highs (morning lows) ware\nforecast in the 110-116 (85-95) degree range across the lower \ndeserts and in the 100-110 (75-85) degree range across the higher \nterrain areas. The increase in temperatures will also result in an\nincrease in HeatRisk, with widespread Extreme HeatRisk forecast\nfor the majority of the CWA by Wednesday. There is high confidence\nalready in the heat forecast for next week. So, the Extreme Heat \nWarning was been extended through next Friday and will likely need\nto be expanded to more areas by the middle of the week.",
